    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Quechua, a family of closely related languages that grew in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in the first millennium CE, became the common language of the Inca empire, which carried it along the Andes from Ecuador to Chile.</li><li>After the Spanish conquest, missionaries used it to preach across the Andes, spreading it further than ever, and it was written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet in grammars, sermons, chronicles and plays.</li><li>Spoken today by millions of people in six countries, it is the most widely spoken Indigenous language family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and words such as llama, condor, puma and quinoa come from it.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>English words such as llama, condor, puma, coca, quinoa and guano come from Quechua, and so does jerky, from ch’arki, the dried meat that sustained Andean travellers.</p></aside>
    </section>

